# the boom hooks that run cellar: setup, input, stepping, drawing, teardown
#
# boom owns the window, the input fold, the renderer and the frame loop. keys
# and panning are read in the fixed tick, where boom gives every edge to
# exactly one tick, and the window's events are folded into the interface's
# input each frame (cellar.gui). the world is stepped on the simulation thread
# (cellar.runner) on the CPU and HashLife, capped at the rate the speed slider
# sets or uncapped, and on the GPU when it can be (cellar.gpu), where the
# generations are recorded with the frame: the capped ones the thread owes, or
# as many a frame as the GPU's budget allows uncapped. each frame builds the
# interface in blit, where the world claims its view beneath every control so
# a press, the wheel and the hovered cell reach it only where nothing of the
# interface is on top, then draws the cells straight from the GPU's pool, the
# world's own overlays (cellar.paint) and the interface over them in one
# overlay pass.
#
# a frame never waits on a step. it holds the world only when the thread is not
# stepping it, or between steps when commands are queued, and then applies them,
# records and releases it before drawing. otherwise it draws the last completed
# generation the thread published, and the panels read what it posted of it
# (world.Shown), never the world itself.

